WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.870
Film Courage: How do you keep an audience
guessing with plot twists and reveals?

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:07.590
Phillip G. Carroll, Jr., Writer/Director:
When I was working on THE HONEYMOON PHASE

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:14.320
had this mindset of the film, it’s like
the Hot Wheel car track where…maybe this

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:20.039
is because I grew up with Hot Wheel cars…where
on the track for the Hot Wheel cars there

00:00:20.039 --> 00:00:25.520
is there’s like these little spinning motors
that will push the car further.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:31.310
It goes down the hill, it reaches the bottom,
it reaches a part where naturally the gravity

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:34.800
would stop the car because it would run to
the end of the track, then there would be

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.461
a motor that would push it along further and
throughout the track there would be all of

00:00:38.461 --> 00:00:43.680
these motors that eventually where it needed
to go next.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:48.700
To me that’s kind of a good analogy for
a script where you have these key scenes in

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:52.980
the story which are your “motors” that
push the story further, so like the attention

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:58.059
grabbing moments because a lot of points of
your story are where your characters are doing

00:00:58.059 --> 00:01:03.039
things that on a surface level maybe aren’t
the most entertaining things to watch but

00:01:03.039 --> 00:01:08.639
are important to the story but you need these
motors to keep the audience guessing and keep

00:01:08.639 --> 00:01:11.139
them engaged.

00:01:11.139 --> 00:01:15.250
In HONEYMOON PHASE we would throughout the
story when I was outlining I would pick points

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:19.689
where I was like Ah…I think we need a motor
here and then come up with a twist or something

00:01:19.689 --> 00:01:23.950
that made sense within the realm of the film
to keep the audience guessing.

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:30.289
You would do that by thinking about the characters
and where they are in that point of the story

00:01:30.289 --> 00:01:35.640
and what their belief is in their present
world and then how do you flip that on its

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:38.079
head by making them question their own reality.

00:01:38.079 --> 00:01:41.780
This is a psychological thriller so I guess
that is why some of that terminology is coming

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:46.850
into play there but the Hot Wheel track is
kind of how I view a story.

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:51.880
Film Courage: And so the loop [of the car
track], that would be toward then end?

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:56.479
Phil: That would be the big twists.

00:01:56.479 --> 00:02:00.039
I think that would be a very Hollywood way
of thinking about it where a lot of blockbuster

00:02:00.039 --> 00:02:06.159
films have their “set-piece” moments,
they would be the moments or the big reveals

00:02:06.159 --> 00:02:10.420
and then in-between you have the scenes that
are important to the story but maybe aren’t

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:14.239
your trailer moments or the things that are
going to leave you like with your jaw dropped

00:02:14.239 --> 00:02:20.500
because you find out that this person is cheating
on this person or that person is the bad one

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:21.520
(the gear moments).

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:28.190
Film Courage: Are there any films that you
love which are from the arthouse genre that

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:29.200
are not Hollywood movies?

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:34.500
Which were really powerful, the way characters
played against one another?

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:36.159
Phil: I’m trying to think.

00:02:36.159 --> 00:02:42.480
Most of the films that I grew up with were
Hollywood films and independent films like

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:50.340
as I matured as a film goer I start appreciating
more independent film as well.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:56.349
Arthouse film was nothing that I was able
to I guess connect with as a viewer.

00:02:56.349 --> 00:02:58.599
And that is not to say that I don’t respect
it as an art form.

00:02:58.599 --> 00:03:04.799
I think it’s beautiful and there is so much
to be said in arthouse movies, it’s just

00:03:04.799 --> 00:03:06.950
as a viewer I could never connect with it.

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:12.049
It’s more of a genre that I respect but
maybe don’t necessarily connect with.

